WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000 align:start position:0% line:0%
Transcriber: Leslie Gauthier
Reviewer: 

00:00:00.121 --> 00:00:03.499
Have you ever reached the end
of what feels like a grueling workday

00:00:03.499 --> 00:00:06.502
only to realize you didn’t actually
accomplish anything?

00:00:06.502 --> 00:00:08.337
That it was just meeting after meeting

00:00:08.337 --> 00:00:10.465
after meeting after meeting
after meeting --

00:00:10.465 --> 00:00:12.759
[The Way We Work]

00:00:12.759 --> 00:00:14.260
(Music)

00:00:15.219 --> 00:00:16.971
As a recovering corporate executive,

00:00:16.971 --> 00:00:19.682
I know we all feel
like our time isn’t our own,

00:00:19.682 --> 00:00:21.934
like other people 
are controlling our calendars

00:00:21.934 --> 00:00:23.895
and we’re simply reacting to their whims.

00:00:23.895 --> 00:00:25.938
But calendar creep isn’t inevitable.

00:00:26.314 --> 00:00:28.483
There's so much in the world
we can't control.

00:00:28.483 --> 00:00:30.234
We can’t control our senior leaders,

00:00:30.234 --> 00:00:32.070
we can’t control our customer demands,

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:34.405
and we certainly can’t control
a global pandemic.

00:00:34.489 --> 00:00:36.741
But we can actually control our time,

00:00:36.866 --> 00:00:38.534
we’ve just forgotten how to do it.

00:00:38.826 --> 00:00:41.162
I’ve come up with five, 
easy-to-implement steps

00:00:41.162 --> 00:00:44.457
that can take your calendar
from working against you

00:00:44.457 --> 00:00:45.958
to working for you.

00:00:46.125 --> 00:00:47.126
And they really work.

00:00:47.126 --> 00:00:48.836
We worked with a big global company

00:00:48.836 --> 00:00:51.798
and asked some of their leaders
to put these tips into practice

00:00:51.798 --> 00:00:52.799
while others didn’t.

00:00:52.799 --> 00:00:53.800
And guess what?

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:57.095
The leaders who used these steps
saw significant hours open up

00:00:57.095 --> 00:00:58.096
on their calendars

00:00:58.096 --> 00:01:00.348
for, you know, actual work.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:01.808
Tip number one:

00:01:01.808 --> 00:01:04.811
Ask yourself, “Do you really
need the meeting?”

00:01:05.228 --> 00:01:08.189
We’re under the illusion that we need
a meeting for everything.

00:01:08.189 --> 00:01:10.775
We think “I need to make sure
so-and-so is OK with this

00:01:10.775 --> 00:01:11.776
so I’ll book time.”

00:01:11.776 --> 00:01:14.737
Or “I’ve got a quick question
on process, I’ll grab a meeting.”

00:01:14.737 --> 00:01:17.573
The reality is for almost half
of the meetings we schedule,

00:01:17.573 --> 00:01:20.827
we could simply pick up the phone
or shoot a text for a quick answer.

00:01:21.035 --> 00:01:22.495
A trick to stop this:

00:01:22.495 --> 00:01:24.497
when you’re thinking of calling a meeting,

00:01:24.497 --> 00:01:26.958
write the invitation first.

00:01:27.208 --> 00:01:30.628
And if you can’t start with a subject line
with an action verb,

00:01:30.628 --> 00:01:32.213
you shouldn’t have the meeting.

00:01:32.463 --> 00:01:34.799
“Decide, finalize, create next steps.”

00:01:34.966 --> 00:01:36.717
Those are reasons to call a meeting.

00:01:36.926 --> 00:01:40.263
“Review,” on the other hand,
isn’t an action verb.

00:01:40.263 --> 00:01:42.515
If you're calling a meeting
to review something,

00:01:42.515 --> 00:01:43.766
send it out ahead of time

00:01:43.766 --> 00:01:45.977
and schedule a 15-minute
meeting for questions.

00:01:46.269 --> 00:01:48.396
That should get Joe
to finally read the deck.

00:01:48.688 --> 00:01:50.398
Related to that action verb,

00:01:50.398 --> 00:01:51.983
if you’re going to call a meeting

00:01:51.983 --> 00:01:54.569
you should be able to create
a clear purpose statement.

00:01:54.569 --> 00:01:57.655
“In this meeting we’re going to decide
boom, boom, boom.

00:01:57.655 --> 00:01:58.698
Come prepared.”

00:01:58.906 --> 00:02:00.366
You don’t need a whole agenda;

00:02:00.366 --> 00:02:01.951
nobody’s going to read it anyway.

00:02:01.951 --> 00:02:05.246
But that purpose statement
is enough  so that when you start,

00:02:05.246 --> 00:02:08.749
everybody is sitting up, paying attention
and focused on the goal.

00:02:09.041 --> 00:02:10.334
Tip number two:

00:02:10.334 --> 00:02:12.920
invite the least number
of people possible.

00:02:13.379 --> 00:02:14.380
Let’s be honest,

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:16.841
most of us invite people
to meetings defensively.

00:02:17.049 --> 00:02:18.759
We know that Raco’s the one we need

00:02:18.759 --> 00:02:20.845
but if Dion doesn’t feel
like he’s involved,

00:02:20.845 --> 00:02:22.013
he’s going to be cranky,

00:02:22.013 --> 00:02:24.307
so you invite him
and then Shannon and then Jane.

00:02:24.307 --> 00:02:26.559
And now we’re wasting
all of these people’s time

00:02:26.559 --> 00:02:29.061
instead of just going
directly to the decision maker.

00:02:29.061 --> 00:02:31.689
It’s time to let go 
of those grade-school fears

00:02:31.689 --> 00:02:35.234
and just invite the people
who are necessary for the objective.

00:02:35.651 --> 00:02:37.486
Everyone else can be informed later.

00:02:37.695 --> 00:02:41.115
Let’s also agree it’s OK 
if we’re not invited to everything.

00:02:41.532 --> 00:02:44.994
Research has found that the optimal size
of a decision-making meeting

00:02:44.994 --> 00:02:46.537
is around five to eight people.

00:02:46.954 --> 00:02:48.497
Any time you're inviting more,

00:02:48.497 --> 00:02:51.375
you're making it less likely
you'll achieve your goal.

00:02:51.626 --> 00:02:53.002
Tip number three:

00:02:53.002 --> 00:02:54.503
make your meetings shorter.

00:02:54.795 --> 00:02:56.172
If you want your time back,

00:02:56.172 --> 00:02:57.548
ditch the hour-long meeting.

00:02:57.673 --> 00:02:59.508
I schedule 30- and 45-minute meetings.

00:02:59.508 --> 00:03:00.635
That’s it, period.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:01.761
Full stop.

00:03:02.053 --> 00:03:03.638
That gives people time to digest,

00:03:03.638 --> 00:03:04.805
figure out next steps,

00:03:04.805 --> 00:03:05.806
then take a breath

00:03:05.806 --> 00:03:08.392
and maybe, I don’t know,
go to the bathroom.

00:03:08.768 --> 00:03:11.520
It stops that horrible
snowball of lateness

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.981
that rolls downhill
over the course of a day.

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:15.399
Tip number four:

00:03:15.399 --> 00:03:17.735
say no to other’s people’s meetings.

00:03:17.902 --> 00:03:21.072
We’re in the habit of saying yes
to every meeting we’re invited to.

00:03:21.155 --> 00:03:24.325
Often we show up out of fear
of missing out, or worse yet, ego.

00:03:24.742 --> 00:03:28.037
Neither of those is a reason
to spend your precious time in a meeting.

00:03:28.037 --> 00:03:29.372
A better way to decide:

00:03:29.372 --> 00:03:33.918
Ask yourself, “Is my opinion absolutely
vital to the purpose of this meeting?”

00:03:34.293 --> 00:03:37.088
Even better, “Does this meeting
move my goals,

00:03:37.088 --> 00:03:39.340
my team’s goals
or my customers’ goals forward?”

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:40.967
If not, just say no.

00:03:41.592 --> 00:03:43.135
Now I know what you’re thinking:

00:03:43.135 --> 00:03:44.720
it’s hard to say no to a meeting.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:46.138
But it really isn’t.

00:03:46.722 --> 00:03:48.849
Simply tell the organizer the truth.

00:03:49.225 --> 00:03:50.726
You know that they’ve got this,

00:03:50.726 --> 00:03:51.727
and if they need you,

00:03:51.727 --> 00:03:52.853
simply give you a ring.

00:03:52.853 --> 00:03:55.481
You can also use the opportunity
to delegate the meeting

00:03:55.481 --> 00:03:57.566
to a high performer
or subject matter expert

00:03:57.566 --> 00:03:59.193
who may be a better choice anyway.

00:03:59.193 --> 00:04:02.446
You can even simply let them know
you have other priorities that week

00:04:02.446 --> 00:04:04.365
and ask if your attendance is necessary.

00:04:04.824 --> 00:04:09.036
All you need to do is communicate
with honesty and clarity.

00:04:09.161 --> 00:04:10.329
Tip number five:

00:04:10.329 --> 00:04:11.706
be ruthless with your time.

00:04:12.164 --> 00:04:14.000
As any flight attendant will tell you,

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.460
you have to put
your own oxygen mask on first.

00:04:16.585 --> 00:04:19.755
It’s the only way
you can be at your best for others,

00:04:19.755 --> 00:04:22.883
so give yourself time
to do the things you need to

00:04:22.883 --> 00:04:24.969
in order to feel like a human being.

00:04:25.052 --> 00:04:28.264
That includes scheduling
blocks of uninterrupted time

00:04:28.264 --> 00:04:30.308
to focus on your own work.

00:04:30.516 --> 00:04:33.019
If you have a project
that going to take you 10 hours

00:04:33.019 --> 00:04:34.687
of really focused time and effort,

00:04:34.687 --> 00:04:36.480
schedule that time in your calendar.

00:04:36.647 --> 00:04:39.150
Try putting in “no-fly zones”
two hours a day,

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:40.276
a few days a week,

00:04:40.276 --> 00:04:43.154
at whatever time
you’re at your most productive.

00:04:44.071 --> 00:04:46.657
You don’t have to make
these changes in a vacuum,

00:04:46.657 --> 00:04:48.159
like it’s some kind of secret.

00:04:48.492 --> 00:04:50.953
You can tell people
that you’re trying something new

00:04:50.953 --> 00:04:52.705
and taking control of your calendar.

00:04:52.747 --> 00:04:55.124
And you do not have to do
everything at once.

00:04:55.291 --> 00:04:57.793
Simply pick one idea and try it.

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:00.212
People will not only understand it,

00:05:00.212 --> 00:05:01.881
but they’ll appreciate it.

00:05:01.964 --> 00:05:03.591
So the only question left is:

00:05:03.591 --> 00:05:06.218
Do you have the courage
to own your own calendar?

00:05:06.552 --> 00:05:07.553
I think you do.

